  "detail": null,
  "summary": "I reconciled all five patients' medication lists against their St. Brigid discharge papers under RBC-TOC-02 and wrote a progress note for each. Four new items came in while I worked (Kim, Stark, Roberts). They changed decisions for all three, so I corrected those orders and wrote an update note for each.\n\n**Walter Kim (pt-0005)**\n- Furosemide changed from 20 mg daily to 40 mg twice daily. The signed addendum overrides the summary's 40 mg daily.\n- Potassium chloride stopped, as the discharge summary says.\n- Omeprazole kept. Pantoprazole was only the hospital's formulary substitute, so I didn't order it.\n- Metoprolol XL, atorvastatin and aspirin are unchanged.\n- Lisinopril: at first I kept it on hold because his 10/21 potassium was 5.4. A repeat lab drawn this morning showed potassium 4.7 and creatinine 1.21, so I resumed it. The follow-up task I'd made you (task-t0003) is no longer needed, but I couldn't close it from here, so please close it.\n- **For you:** an unsigned draft addendum from a night hospitalist says furosemide 60 mg twice daily. Only signed addenda count, so I left him on 40 mg twice daily. You may want to confirm the dose with Dr. Boateng.\n\n**Terence Stark (pt-0004)**\n- Carvedilol: I first raised it to 12.5 mg twice daily per the summary. A signed addendum this morning reversed that because of a slow heart rate (48–52) and lightheadedness, so it's back to his home 6.25 mg twice daily.\n- Aspirin and insulin 70/30 (18 units twice daily) are unchanged.\n- **For you:** he filled atorvastatin 80 mg for only about 24% of the 180 days before admission (last fill 3/4), so he likely isn't taking it as prescribed. I made no change and sent you a message.\n- **For you:** outside pharmacy records show glipizide 5 mg from Dr. Desai (Valley Endocrinology), which the discharge summary doesn't mention. I didn't change or order it. Taking it with insulin raises the risk of low blood sugar, so please decide whether he should continue; I sent you an urgent message.\n\n**Earnest Roberts (pt-0044)**\n- Insulin glargine, insulin lispro and rosuvastatin are unchanged.\n- Lisinopril: I resumed it because his 10/22 creatinine (1.08) met the discharge condition. A potassium of 5.5 then came in this morning, so I put it back on hold. You have a task due 10/26 to repeat the potassium and decide on lisinopril.\n\n**Gerald Whitcomb (pt-0069)**\n- Lisinopril 20 mg resumed: his 10/21 creatinine was 1.12 (condition was under 1.5) and potassium 4.4.\n- Amlodipine and atorvastatin are unchanged.\n- The potassium check planned for 3 days after discharge hasn't come in yet.\n\n**Lorraine Pruitt (pt-0067)**\n- Metformin ER stays on hold. Her only creatinine (1.21) was drawn in the hospital, so it doesn't count as the post-discharge lab the summary requires.\n- You have a task due 10/24, when her post-discharge blood test is expected.\n- Amlodipine and atorvastatin are unchanged.\n\nThere are no other open items for these five patients in your results or messages queues.",
